---
// Frequently asked questions: how to check the record (moved here from the home page), what the SHA-256 and its
// pattern are, how to follow new items (including what the calendar feeds do and how to subscribe), and where the
// methodology, the data and the report form are. Short answers that link to the page with the full rule, so nothing
// here can drift from methodology.astro or data.astro. Static, no script (AC12).
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Glyph from '../components/Glyph.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE_URL } from '../lib/methodology.js';
import { byDecisionNewestFirst, labelText } from '../lib/radar.js';

// The worked hash example is a real one: the first source of the latest decision, as the home page used to show.
const example = byDecisionNewestFirst(await getCollection('actions'))[0]?.data.documents[0];
const calendarUrl = `${SITE_URL}/radar.ics`;
---

<Base title="FAQ">
  <Fragment slot="masthead">
    <h1>Frequently asked questions</h1>
    <p class="lede">How to check what we publish, and how to follow it.</p>
  </Fragment>

  <section aria-labelledby="check-the-record" class="faq">
    <h2 id="check-the-record">How can I check the record?</h2>
    <ol class="trust-list">
      <li>A person reviews every item before it is published.</li>
      <li>Each item links its official source and says when we retrieved it, in Eastern time.</li>
      <li>We keep a copy and print its SHA-256, so anyone can confirm it hasn't changed.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The SHA-256 is under <strong>More details</strong> beside each source on an item or
      jurisdiction page.
    </p>
    {example && (
      <figure class="hash-example">
        <Glyph sha256={example.sha256} />
        <figcaption>
          <code>{example.sha256}</code>
          <span class="muted">
            The pattern is drawn from the hash itself, so two copies with the same hash always look
            alike.
          </span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    )}
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="check-a-hash" class="faq">
    <h2 id="check-a-hash">What is a SHA-256, and how do I check one?</h2>
    <p>
      A SHA-256 is a 64-character fingerprint of a file's exact bytes. Change one byte and the
      fingerprint changes completely. If the file you download from the source gives the same
      SHA-256 we print, it is the same copy we used.
    </p>
    <p>Download the source, run one of these, and compare the result with the hash we print.</p>
    <pre><code># Linux
sha256sum downloaded-file
# macOS
shasum -a 256 downloaded-file
# Windows
certutil -hashfile downloaded-file SHA256</code></pre>
    <p>
      A different hash can mean the government changed or replaced the file after we retrieved it.
      If you find one, please <a href="/report/">report it</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="labels" class="faq">
    <h2 id="labels">
      What do “{labelText('confirmed_official')}” and “{labelText('reported_news')}” mean?
    </h2>
    <p>
      Confirmed items rest on at least one official record from the government's own site. Reported
      items rest on a news report until we keep an official record that shows them.{' '}
      <a href="/methodology/#labels">The full rule</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="coverage" class="faq">
    <h2 id="coverage">Why don't some places show upcoming meetings?</h2>
    <p>
      Some jurisdictions are covered for decisions only: we record what they decided, but we don't
      watch their agendas, so they have no upcoming meetings and no calendar.{' '}
      <a href="/methodology/#coverage">What each level of coverage means</a>. If something isn't in
      the ledger, that is not evidence it didn't happen.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="calendar" class="faq">
    <h2 id="calendar">Can I get upcoming meetings in my calendar?</h2>
    <p>
      Yes. The Hearing Radar is published as a calendar feed (iCal, a <code>.ics</code> address).
      Subscribed to in a calendar app, it adds each upcoming meeting we list, and when a meeting is
      moved or cancelled your calendar updates by itself. Each watched jurisdiction's page has its
      own feed, too.
    </p>
    <p>Subscribe with this address:</p>
    <pre><code>{calendarUrl}</code></pre>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Google Calendar</strong> (on a computer): Other calendars, +, From URL, then paste
        the address.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Apple Calendar</strong>: on a Mac, File, New Calendar Subscription; on an iPhone, in
        Settings under Calendar accounts, Add Account, Other, Add Subscribed Calendar.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Outlook</strong>: Add calendar, Subscribe from web.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="muted">
      Opening the address in a browser downloads the file instead. Importing that file adds a
      one-time copy that won't update, so subscribe with the address. Calendar apps check for
      changes on their own schedule, often every few hours.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="dates-ahead" class="faq">
    <h2 id="dates-ahead">Can I see dates before an agenda posts?</h2>
    <p>
      Yes, when the record already states them. <a href="/ahead/">Coming up on the record</a> lists
      the date a decision ends, such as a moratorium's expiry date, and the date a document gives
      for a matter's next step, such as the meeting a vote was continued to. Only dates stated in an
      archived document are listed: nothing is worked out from a number of days, a meeting schedule
      or a news report. A body can act on a matter before or after these dates.
    </p>
    <p>
      They are a calendar too: <code>{`${SITE_URL}/ahead.ics`}</code>, and one for each covered
      place on its page. The <a href="/feed.xml">feed</a> adds a reminder 30 days and 7 days before
      each date, and an entry the day after a date that passes with nothing recorded for it yet.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="follow" class="faq">
    <h2 id="follow">Is there another way to follow new items?</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="/feed.xml">RSS feed</a> has one entry for each new, changed or cancelled meeting.
      For spreadsheets and analysis, the meetings and decisions are downloadable on the{' '}
      <a href="/data/">data page</a>, licensed CC BY 4.0.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="mistake" class="faq">
    <h2 id="mistake">I found a mistake. What do I do?</h2>
    <p>
      Use the <a href="/report/">report form</a>. How reports are handled, and every fix we make, is
      on the <a href="/methodology/#corrections">methodology page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
